WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Autophagy wraps cell material in carriers and sends it to lysosomes for recycling.

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.680
Those carriers are autophagosomes.

3
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.160
Yet two cells can hold the same large pile.

4
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.020
Is autophagy faster in both?

5
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.020
One makes and clears them quickly.

6
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.220
The other makes them slowly, but its exit is blocked.

7
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.020
A still picture cannot distinguish those stories.

8
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:35.573
The endoplasmic reticulum, or ER, uses helpers to retry proteins that have not

9
00:00:35.573 --> 00:00:36.753
reached their working shape.

10
00:00:37.973 --> 00:00:39.233
This is a first picture.

11
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:45.993
In the ER, many failed proteins are returned to the cytosol, tagged with ubiquitin,

12
00:00:46.353 --> 00:00:48.073
and destroyed by the proteasome.

13
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:51.573
Ubiquitin is that small protein tag.

14
00:00:51.573 --> 00:00:57.673
The proteasome is a capped barrel that unfolds and destroys tagged individual

15
00:00:57.673 --> 00:00:58.673
proteins.

16
00:00:59.383 --> 00:01:02.963
Now a damaged mitochondrion reaches the same station.

17
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:06.743
It cannot enter the proteasome's narrow barrel.

18
00:01:08.183 --> 00:01:10.923
Macroautophagy uses a different physical solution.

19
00:01:12.163 --> 00:01:17.003
A blue membrane cup grows around the cargo and closes into an autophagosome.

20
00:01:18.223 --> 00:01:23.883
In macroautophagy, autophagosomes carry bulk cytoplasm and whole organelles to

21
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:30.483
lysosomes. The carrier fuses with a lysosome, whose acidic interior digests the

22
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:32.203
cargo into reusable blocks.

23
00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:38.923
Starvation reduces building blocks arriving from outside the cell, so the reuse bin

24
00:01:38.923 --> 00:01:45.843
begins to empty. More cytoplasm now enters autophagosomes and reaches lysosomes.

25
00:01:47.223 --> 00:01:50.103
Digestion sends recovered blocks back into the bin.

26
00:01:51.463 --> 00:01:56.683
During starvation, autophagy recycles cell material into reusable building blocks.

27
00:01:57.903 --> 00:02:03.583
That recycling may be one reason autophagy-defective yeast lose viability faster

28
00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:05.243
during nitrogen starvation.

29
00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:09.267
Now watch the carrier pool between its two gates.

30
00:02:10.547 --> 00:02:15.327
For a defined cellular pool, change equals arrival minus clearance over the

31
00:02:15.327 --> 00:02:18.607
interval. Matching rates keep the pool level.

32
00:02:20.007 --> 00:02:21.387
Faster arrival makes it grow.

33
00:02:22.907 --> 00:02:24.007
Slower clearance does too.

34
00:02:25.247 --> 00:02:30.127
For a defined cellular pool, a larger pool can mean faster arrival or slower

35
00:02:30.127 --> 00:02:34.927
clearance. We need an experiment that separates those possibilities.

36
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:42.050
LC3-II is a protein form attached to autophagosome membranes, so it marks the

37
00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:48.690
carriers. Bafilomycin blocks lysosomal acidification, closing the exit during this

38
00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:53.830
short assay. Now two LC3-II pools start equally high.

39
00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:00.470
After bafilomycin, which pool rises more: the one with fast arrival or slow arrival?

40
00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:09.703
The fast-arrival pool rises more after clearance stops.

41
00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:15.163
Its purple gauge climbs steeply while the slow-arrival gauge barely moves.

42
00:03:16.543 --> 00:03:22.103
Under a complete lysosomal block that leaves carrier formation unchanged, the extra

43
00:03:22.103 --> 00:03:26.403
LC3-II estimates lysosomal turnover over the interval.

44
00:03:27.583 --> 00:03:30.783
The equal starting amounts could not reveal that difference.

45
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.920
Return to the two cells with equal carrier piles.

46
00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.160
Their visible amounts matched, but their flows did not.

47
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.120
Closing the exit exposed the difference in arrival.

48
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:48.860
The same question works across the recycling map, even when the cargo and route

49
00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.200
change. Wherever material piles up, ask two questions.

50
00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.820
How fast does it arrive?

51
00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.340
How fast does it leave?

52
00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:58.420
How fast does it leave?
